A CREATE that changed only in its spacing (or a comment inside it) is
still a change: SQLite keeps each CREATE as written, and roux compares it
to the byte rather than guess which differences matter. The table is
rebuilt, which costs a copy of its rows and nothing else.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, -- the rowid
  name TEXT NOT NULL
) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (
      id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, -- the rowid
      name TEXT NOT NULL
    ) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name") SELECT "id", "name" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"
